le/PDP-endian), four bytes (UTF-32, prebuilt), no BOM (prebuilt), the whole\ntext as one BIG integer, or a knob on an older day's codec (code-unit escapes byte-swapped, bit order, alphabet).\nD21 (\"A familiar wrapper. Something compressed inside.\" / \"Under Pressure\") opened a new family, COMPRESSION: GZIP\ninside standard padded BASE64 (`H4sIAAAAAAACA...`, header `1f8b0800 00000000 0203`); Mastery line 21 = each 36-char\nfragment its own gzip member. \"Familiar wrapper\" = an already-seen codec as the OUTER layer. Expect the D19->D20\npattern again (\"same wrapper, other squeeze\") -- and D22 delivered it: \"Another familiar wrapper. This one needs a little\ninflation.\" / \"Deflated Ego\" = ZLIB (`78da`, level 9) inside std padded BASE64 (`eNo..`/`eNp..`/`eNr..`); Mastery line\n22 = each 20-char fragment its own zlib stream. The D21 solver's `_b64z` (magic sniff incl. `\\x78`) passed it unchanged.\nRHYTHM since D19: day N opens a family, day N+1 turns ONE knob (D19 LE -> D20 BE, D21 gzip -> D22 zlib). D23-D25 each\nopened a NEW family instead: D23 \"The alphabet got bigger. Adobe might recognize the neighbors.\" = ASCII85 `<~...~>`\n(Mastery line 23 own delimiters); D24 \"Count carefully. The comma knows where to stop.\" / \"String Theory\" = DJB\nNETSTRINGS `1:N,1:i,...` (one char per frame, payload `,`/`:` framed as `1:,,`/`1::,`; line 24 `1:o,1:n,...`); D25\n\"Every character has a point. A code point, anyway.\" / \"You Have a Point\" = Unicode CODE POINTS `U+004E U+0069 ...`\n(UPPERCASE, 4 hex digits, single spaces, 762 B = 7 B per char; Mastery line 25 = 5 tokens per fragment), one layer,\nplain text inside -- a return to the D7-D9 CHARACTER-NOTATION family (D9 was `\"\\u00XX\"`), predicted here as row 22\n(prebuilt `uplus_dec`, single CORRECT ~27 s). Only a \"Same ...\"/\"Another ...\" prompt has signalled a knob day.\nD26 (\"A very small language has quite a lot to say.\" / \"Food for Thought\" = brain food) opened a NEW family,\nESOTERIC LANGUAGES: a BRAINFUCK program whose output is the sentence -- only `+ - .`, one cell, each char = a run of one\nsign equal to the delta from the previous char, then `.` (3406 B, ~31 B per char; Mastery line 26 = the same over a\n5-char tail, 166-217 B). Row 83 had rated it LOW (\"program, not encoding\"): the pun named a LANGUAGE, not a codec. The\nUnicode/notation queue (rows 173-182, all built) did not come. Mastery broke on it: the solver's runtime DAYS encoder\n(absolute `+`*ord) did not round-trip the GM's delta bytes -> 64.9 s timeout (see mastery-line-routing).\nD27 (\"Ook. Ook? Ook! Someone seems chatty.\" / \"Monkey Business\") was the BF KNOB predicted here, (a) first: OOK!, the\nexact D26 one-cell delta program re-spelled as `Ook.`/`Ook!` word PAIRS (single-spaced, no `Ook?` used, ~314 B per\nchar, 33929 B; Mastery line 27 = 1672-2172 B printing a 4-5 char tail). A knob day WITHOUT a \"Same\"/\"Another\" cue:\nthe prompt quoted the dialect's tokens. Prebuilt `warmups_codecs.bfd_dec` (row 183) covered it; single CORRECT ~33 s,\nTT/Mastery first try (Mastery solve 34.9 s: the solver's own DAYS[27] encoder again was not GM-faithful).\nD28+ forecast, in order: (a) a THIRD esolang day -- another spelling or another tiny language: unknown word triads /\nunseparated or emoji token maps (row 194), animal-word languages (Chicken, row 193 -- \"monkey\" -> next animal),\nBrain-Flak (`()[]{}<>`, row 192), bit-level BF (Boolfuck, row 197), prefix-code Spoon (row 195), the built esolang rows\n184-191 (Deadfish, Whitespace, Befunge, JSFuck, Chef, stack VM, Malbolge); (b) a program whose OUTPUT is itself a seen\ncodec, or a program under a seen wrapper (row 196: always re-decode interpreter output); (c) a new family for the\nlast days (D28-D31). All keyless and RUNNABLE: pure-python, step-capped interpreters + crib slug gate. Snapshot\n2026-10-27 (after D27; every earlier prebuild gap is built; new queue rows 192-200).\n\nStatus legend: `seen Dn` = GM-confirmed day codec (`lib.warmups